title: Event log
kind: overview
aliases: log history
summary: Every write is a revision in a tree of operations kept beside the data — so any change can be audited, undone, reverted in place, or navigated back to.

Every change to a repository's metadata is recorded in its ''event log'', in the same
store and the same transaction as the change itself. The log is a ''tree'' of
operations, which is what makes it more than an audit trail:

* ''auditing'' — the full history of any metarecord ([[Reading the log]]);
* ''undo'' and ''redo'' — take back the newest change ''you'' made, and take that back in turn;
* ''revert'' — undo one past revision or operation ''in place'', by writing what it replaced as a new revision;
* ''rollback'' — move the current state to any point of the tree, past or future;
* ''non-destructive branching'' — after moving back, a new write starts a new branch; the "future" left behind is not lost.

The data always reflects exactly the operation ''HEAD'' points at: replaying the
operations from the root to HEAD reproduces it ([[History tree and HEAD]]). History is
lost only by an explicit prune, or by the retention limit (the last 200 revisions by
default).

